/* System tab — smooth scrolling for mice */
"settings.smoothscroll.section"           = "Smooth Scrolling";
"settings.smoothscroll.label"             = "Silky scroll for mouse wheel";
"settings.smoothscroll.distance.label"    = "Scroll distance";
"settings.smoothscroll.duration.label"    = "Animation length";
"settings.smoothscroll.footer"            = "Affects: mouse-wheel scrolling (pages and lists).\nReplaces Mac's chunky line-by-line motion with an animated, pixel-precise scroll — fixes the jerky feel when scrolling back. Trackpad and Magic Mouse gestures are left untouched. Cmd / Opt / Ctrl / Shift + wheel keep their original step behavior so zooming still works.\n• Scroll distance: pixels per wheel notch (1.00x ≈ 36 px; raise to scroll more per click, lower for finer control)\n• Animation length: how long one notch glides (80 ms = snappy, 220 ms ≈ Mos default, 600 ms = floaty)\nRequires Accessibility permission.";

/* System tab — linear pointer (no acceleration) */
"settings.linearpointer.section"          = "Pointer Linearization";
"settings.linearpointer.label"            = "Disable mouse pointer acceleration";
"settings.linearpointer.speed.label"      = "Pointer speed";
"settings.linearpointer.footer"           = "Affects: the mouse cursor's overall movement (not the wheel).\nMac applies an acceleration curve by default — moving slowly travels less, moving fast travels far. That's awkward for precision aiming. With this on, motion is linear: 1 cm of mouse travel is always the same cursor distance, like Windows / LinearMouse. Trackpad acceleration is preserved.\n• Pointer speed: overall sensitivity once linearized (1.0x = system current; 1.5x faster for big displays, 0.7x steadier for fine work)\nOriginal settings are restored when the toggle is turned off; if you plug in a new mouse while it's on, toggle it off and back on to apply.";

